{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794426878,"nonce":"A8aNx0czDEi2EzThJlBf2jOC0UQ=","message":"Owner decision 2026-11-12: option A, per-ServiceAccount identity. No server-side minting (B) for now; revisit only if many teams set this up themselves and a Policy they must remember becomes the weak point.\n\nScope of this ticket now:\n1. Docs page: an IRSA (and Pod Identity) setup for pushing to ECR from Scarab. A ServiceAccount (e.g. scarab-ecr-push) whose IAM role allows only ecr:GetAuthorizationToken plus push on named repositories, cross-account through the registry's repository policy; IRSA removed from the shared step ServiceAccount; a placement profile that sets spec.serviceAccountName; an org run.create Policy that denies the profile unless project, ref and !trigger.fork match (and steps[].origin once build is a library, 736e397).\n2. lib:thulasi-ram/build (ADR-0018 amendment, PR #178): an optional ECR input. An init container, the only container given the identity (eks.amazonaws.com/skip-containers on the build container), mints the ECR password into a tmpfs DOCKER_CONFIG, so the build container holds a 12-hour ECR-only password and not the role's token. Check whether EKS Pod Identity has an equivalent of skip-containers.\n3. A test that the Policy refuses the profile for a fork PR and another project.\n\nDepends on: a75eacb and df660b6 (in progress on fix/build-fork-and-context), d2b711b (strategic merge, so a profile can add an init container without replacing lists).","files":null}]}